.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm libnvrm
library lists the NVIDIA GPUs in the machine and describes each one, through
the Resource Manager (RM) of the NVIDIA kernel module.
It uses only the RM escape ioctls on
.Pa /dev/nvidiactl ;
it needs no Linux compatibility layer.
See
.Xr nvrm 7
for the model.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_enumerate
selects a backend, opens the control device, checks that the kernel module is
the RM version the library was built for, lists the probed GPUs, attaches them,
queries each one and limits the descriptors for
.Xr capsicum 4 .
.Fa opts
may be
.Dv NULL ;
.Va opts->backend
names a backend
.Pq Dq rm No or Dq fake .
The backend is chosen in this order:
.Bl -enum -offset indent -compact
.It
.Va opts->backend ,
if given;
.It
else the environment variable
.Ev NVRM_BACKEND ,
which is ignored in a set-user-ID or set-group-ID process;
.It
else the backend with the highest score that is usable on the machine.
The fake backend is never chosen this way.
.El
On failure
.Fa *ctxp
is
.Dv NULL
and
.Fa err ,
if not
.Dv NULL ,
holds one line saying why.
.Pp
.Va opts->flags
selects privileges.
Zero-initialize the structure
.Pq Li "nvrm_enum_opts o = { 0 };\&"
with no flag set, or with
.Fa opts
.Dv NULL ,
the descriptors keep only the RM control and free escapes, which is all that
reading device information needs.
A caller that will allocate RM objects after
.Xr cap_enter 2
sets
.Dv NVRM_ENUM_ALLOW_ALLOC
before the limit is applied: Capsicum rights can only be narrowed, never
widened, so they cannot be asked for later.
The enumeration descriptors never carry a mapping right, with or without the
flag.
GPU memory, its virtual addresses and CPU mappings are made through a client
of their own:
.Xr nvrm_mem 3 ,
with
.Dv NVRM_OPEN_ALLOW_MAP
for CPU mappings.
A flag bit the library does not know makes
.Fn nvrm_enumerate
fail with
.Dv NVRM_E_INVAL .
.Pp
No GPU attached is not an error:
.Fn nvrm_enumerate
returns 0 and
.Fn nvrm_device_count
returns 0.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_close
releases everything, including the RM objects.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_device_get
returns the device at
.Fa index ,
or
.Dv NULL .
.Fn nvrm_device_info_get
returns its description, a
.Vt nvrm_device_info .
Every member is an
.Vt nvrm_u64 ,
.Vt nvrm_str ,
.Vt nvrm_list
or
.Vt nvrm_version_field .
The first three contain a value, a reason code and a source.
A value is valid only when
.Fn NVRM_HAVE
is true; otherwise
.Va reason
says why it is not
.Pq see Fn nvrm_reason_id
and no number is made up.
A version field contains a parsed
.Vt nvrm_version
and the source of its string; its
.Va available
member selects between the numeric parts and the string reason identifier.
.Pp
The
.Li compute_capability_major
and
.Li compute_capability_minor
members come only from the architecture/implementation table.
With no table row they are unavailable with reason
.Li family_unknown ;
an alias or the raw RM SM code is never presented as compute capability.
The raw code remains in
.Li rm_sm_version .
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_device_refresh
queries the GPU again through the descriptors that are already open.
It works in capability mode.
It returns
.Dv NVRM_E_IO
when a system call to the driver failed (the affected values then carry the
reason
.Li os_call_failed ) ,
and 0 otherwise; a value the RM itself refuses is not an error of the call.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_info_nfields
and
.Fn nvrm_info_field
describe the members of
.Vt nvrm_device_info
(group, name, kind, whether it reads in hex); the JSON output, the schema and
the text view of
.Xr nvrm-info 8
all walk this one table.
.Pp
The device
.Li versions
group contains
.Li vbios
and
.Li gsp_firmware ,
both with source
.Li rm .
The VBIOS version combines the revision and OEM revision returned by separate
RM BIOS-information controls, formats five two-digit hexadecimal fields, and
parses that string in base 16.
The GSP firmware version comes from the RM GSP-features control and is parsed
in base 10 only when that control marks it valid.
A failed control gives reason
.Li rm_control_failed ;
When the RM does not run on the GPU System Processor, or does not mark its
firmware version valid, the reason is
.Li rm_not_supported ,
and an unreadable version string gives
.Li version_unparsed .
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_backend_name
names the backend in use.
.Fn nvrm_rm_running_version
returns the legacy short version of the running kernel module, or why it is
unknown.
.Fn nvrm_driver_version
returns that same running version as a parsed
.Vt nvrm_version
with source
.Li os .
When the kernel provides separate integer and suffix version sysctls, they must
agree with the parsed version string; a disagreement makes this version
unavailable with reason
.Li version_inconsistent
while preserving its string.
.Fn nvrm_rm_api_version
returns the version answer to the RM query with source
.Li rm .
.Fn nvrm_built_for_version
returns the RM version against which the library was built.
.Fn nvrm_library_version
returns the library API version, currently
.Li 2.0.0 .
.Fn nvrm_symbol_version
returns the library symbol version, currently the string
.Li NVRM_2.0
with integer parts 2 and 0.
.Fn nvrm_schema_version
returns the JSON schema version, currently
.Li 2.4
with integer parts 2 and 4.
The built-for, library, symbol and schema versions have source
.Li derived .
The schema version is also the
.Li versions.schema
object in JSON output.
The context-owned pointers remain valid until
.Fn nvrm_close ;
the other three point to process-lifetime values initialized once.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_strerror
and
.Fn nvrm_error_id
give text and a stable identifier for an error code;
.Fn nvrm_reason_id
and
.Fn nvrm_reason_text
for a reason a value is unavailable;
.Fn nvrm_reason_from_id
is the inverse of
.Fn nvrm_reason_id
for every reason except
.Dv NVRM_R_NONE :
it returns the reason code of an identifier, or \-1 for the identifier of
.Dv NVRM_R_NONE ,
an unknown identifier or a null pointer;
.Fn nvrm_source_name
names the layer that supplied a value and
.Fn nvrm_cap_name
a capability flag by its stable index.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_json_document
writes the whole description as one JSON line, schema
.Li nvrm-info/1 .
The related
.Fn nvrm_json_header ,
.Fn nvrm_json_device ,
.Fn nvrm_json_end
and
.Fn nvrm_json_error
write the records of the line stream.
.Fn nvrm_json_schema
writes the JSON Schema of all of them; it is generated from the same table as
the output.
Each returns 0, or \-1 if writing failed.
.Pp
Document and header records contain a top-level
.Li versions
object with
.Li driver ,
.Li rm_api ,
.Li built_for ,
.Li library ,
.Li library_symbols
and
.Li schema
members.
An available member has this shape (the suffix member is omitted when empty):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"available":true,"string":"580.178.04","major":580,
 "minor":178,"patch":4,"parts":[580,178,4],"source":"os"}
.Ed
.Pp
An unavailable member retains its raw or empty string and has no numeric parts:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"available":false,"string":"N/A","reason_id":"version_unparsed",
 "reason":"the version string has no readable numeric scheme","source":"os"}
.Ed
.Pp
The legacy
.Li built_for_rm_version
and
.Li running_rm_version
members retain their original shapes.
.Ss Version parsing
The freestanding header
.In nvrm/nvrm_version.h
defines
.Vt nvrm_version
and the static inline functions
.Fn nvrm_version_parse ,
.Fn nvrm_version_find
and
.Fn nvrm_version_format .
They perform no allocation, stdio or libc calls.
The type preserves the source in
.Va string ,
exposes up to six integer
.Va parts ,
their digit
.Va width
and count
.Va nparts ,
and any
.Va suffix .
.Va available
selects between those values and the string
.Va reason .
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_version_parse
accepts base 10 or 16, an input string of at most 255 bytes, at most six parts,
and parts that fit in
.Vt uint32_t .
Leading zeroes affect only the preserved width, so
.Li 04
is integer 4.
Missing fixed parts read as zero, while
.Va nparts
records how many were present.
A suffix starts at
.Ql -
or
.Ql +
and is retained with that delimiter, up to 63 bytes.
For example,
.Li 1.2.3-rc1
has parts 1, 2 and 3 and suffix
.Li -rc1 .
Empty, non-numeric, malformed, overlong or overflowing input is unavailable
with reason
.Li version_unparsed ;
its source string is retained as far as the bounded string allows.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_version_find
scans a banner from left to right and returns the first whitespace-separated
token that starts with a digit and parses in base 10 with at least two parts.
It returns unavailable with
.Li version_unparsed
when the banner has no such token.
.Pp
.Fn nvrm_version_format
preserves every accepted parsed string exactly.
For a caller-built available value whose
.Va string
is empty, it reconstructs the string from the parts, widths and suffix.
Formatting therefore round-trips parsed input, and parsing a formatted
caller-built value returns the same parts.
Parse and find return 0 for an available version and \-1 otherwise.
Format returns 0 on success and \-1 for an invalid value or insufficient buffer.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn nvrm_enumerate
returns 0 or one of
.Dv NVRM_E_NOBACKEND ,
.Dv NVRM_E_OPEN ,
.Dv NVRM_E_VERSION ,
.Dv NVRM_E_IO ,
.Dv NVRM_E_NOMEM ,
.Dv NVRM_E_INVAL ,
.Dv NVRM_E_CAPSICUM .
.Dv NVRM_E_NODEV
is no longer returned: a machine with the driver loaded and no GPU gives 0 and
a context with no devices (a caller that tested for
.Dv NVRM_E_NODEV
now takes the success path with a count of 0).
The code stays defined so that existing programs still build.
.Fn nvrm_strerror
and
.Fn nvrm_error_id
turn the code into text and a stable machine identifier.
The memory calls of
.Xr nvrm_mem 3
also return
.Dv NVRM_E_RM ,
.Dv NVRM_E_DENIED ,
.Dv NVRM_E_NOSLOT ,
.Dv NVRM_E_RANGE ,
.Dv NVRM_E_NOTSUP
and
.Dv NVRM_E_BUSY ;
its ERRORS section says when.
.Sh THREADS AND FORK
A context and its devices are not safe for concurrent use.
.Fn nvrm_device_refresh
rewrites in place the information that
.Fn nvrm_device_info_get
handed out, so use one context per thread, or a lock of your own.
Only the registry of backends is shared by every context: the built-in
backends are registered once, with
.Xr pthread_once 3 ,
however many threads reach it first, so the library links
.Lb libthr
and a program that links it statically needs
.Fl l Ns Cm thr .
Nothing else is promised across threads.
The fake backend keeps process-wide test state: use it from one thread.
.Pp
The control descriptor is close-on-exec but survives
.Xr fork 2 ,
and the RM client belongs to the open file, which a child shares with its
parent.
A child must not use a context except to call
.Fn nvrm_close .
The library records the process that enumerated and frees RM objects only
there: in a forked child
.Fn nvrm_close
releases the child's copy of the descriptor and the memory and leaves the
parent's client alone.
The library counts the calls of
.Xr fork 2
in the process, so a process that is given the identifier of a parent that has
exited is not taken for it.
.Xr vfork 2
and
.Xr rfork 2
run no such handler: a child made with them must not call
.Fn nvrm_close ,
or must
.Xr execve 2
first, which closes the descriptor.
.Sh STABILITY
This interface is provisional.
The symbol versions
.Li NVRM_1.0
and its additive successors
.Li NVRM_1.1 ,
.Li NVRM_1.2
and
.Li NVRM_1.3 ,
and the structures behind them, are frozen at the first package release and not
before; a future version may still reshape them.
The memory calls are
.Li NVRM_1.1 ,
API minor 1.
The version calls and
.Fn nvrm_reason_from_id
are
.Li NVRM_1.2 ,
API minor 2; a program that uses them does not load against a library that has
only
.Li NVRM_1.0
or
.Li NVRM_1.1 .
.Pp
The calls that report the version of the interface itself
.Fn nvrm_library_version ,
.Fn nvrm_symbol_version
and
.Fn nvrm_schema_version
are
.Li NVRM_2.0 ,
API major 2: their answers changed with the major, so a program that reads
them needs the new node.
The published values changed with that major: the family of a GPU is a chip
family (the same name the driver publishes) and not the marketing name of the
generation, so a program that compares the family with a name of its own must
be built against the new major.
.Pp
The field table grew after
.Li NVRM_1.0 ,
so
.Fn nvrm_info_nfields
and
.Fn nvrm_info_field
have two versions.
A program linked against
.Li NVRM_1.0
sees exactly the rows of that version, at the same indexes and in the same
order, and never a row of a kind it does not know (the version rows).
New links bind to the
.Li NVRM_1.2
versions, which return the whole table; every row added after 1.0 comes last,
so a later addition never moves an index.
.Pp
The channel calls are
.Li NVRM_1.3 ,
API minor 3
(see
.Xr nvrm_chan 3 ).
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width ".Ev NVRM_FAKE_PROFILE"
